# Concurrent Gradle

Never run two full Gradle builds in the same checkout — they contend on `build/` and `.gradle` (especially painful on Windows).

## Before a long run

```bash
./gradlew --status
```

`BUSY` means another build may already be using a daemon. Idle daemons are fine to share across **different** checkouts.

## When the daemon is BUSY (sequential agent work)

**Wait for it to finish** — keep doing non-Gradle work (read, edit, write tests), poll `./gradlew --status` until `IDLE`, then run. Do not report the task blocked or skip tests because Gradle was busy.

The worktree escape hatch is for **genuinely parallel long runs planned as such** (e.g. game `:desktop:run` in one tree, tests in another). Do **not** create a worktree just to avoid waiting a couple of minutes on a shared checkout. A worktree run also needs dirty changes copied over — usually more cost than the wait.

Other sessions may modify the same tree: at the end of a task, report only the files you changed; say plainly if other diffs came from elsewhere.

## Parallel work → separate worktree

```bash
git worktree add ../iatdb-<purpose> <branch>
cd ../iatdb-<purpose>
./gradlew :core:test
```

Windows: `gradlew.bat`. Keep the game (`:desktop:run`) in one tree and tests in another.

## Do not

- Start a second `./gradlew` in the same tree while another build or IDE sync is writing `build/`
- Rely on `--project-cache-dir` alone — it does not isolate `build/` output
- Use a separate `GRADLE_USER_HOME` unless you need daemon isolation (duplicates caches)

`org.gradle.parallel=true` only parallelizes tasks **inside one build**, not multiple CLI runs.
